---
/* The documents that were in the house all along.
   Built 16 September 2026, after David asked whether this archive had ever
   looked in his own scanned folder. It had not. Everything on this page came
   out of it in twenty minutes, and two of the items had been sitting on the
   work list for a fortnight as things to go and find. */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import marriage from "../data/marriage-1911.json";
import notices from "../data/death-notices-read.json";
import passport from "../data/pablo-armando-passport.json";
import dn from "../data/death-notice-1950.json";
import memory from "../data/family-memory.json";
import llorens from "../data/llorens-1872.json";
import ged from "../data/gedcom-current.json";
import ascent from "../data/brayley-ascent.json";
import conv from "../data/brayley-convergence.json";
const M = Object.fromEntries(marriage.map((r) => [r.field, r]));
const N = Object.fromEntries(notices.map((r) => [r.field, r]));
const D = Object.fromEntries(dn.map((r) => [r.field, r]));
const L = Object.fromEntries(llorens.map((r) => [r.field, r]));
const probes = conv.filter((r) => r.hits && /^\d+$/.test(String(r.hits).trim()));
const convFind = conv.filter((r) => r.probe && r.probe !== "finding" && r.probe !== "what_would_join_them"
  && !/^\d+$/.test(String(r.hits || "").trim()) && r.hits);
const convJoin = conv.find((r) => r.probe === "what_would_join_them");
const forRows = llorens.filter((r) => r.field.startsWith("for_"));
const againstRows = llorens.filter((r) => r.field.startsWith("against_"));
const just = passport.filter((r) => r.field.startsWith("justificativos") ||
  ["photograph", "signature", "fingerprint"].includes(r.field));
const em = (t) => (t || "").replace(/\*\*\* (.+?) \*\*\*/g, "<strong>$1</strong>");
const row = (k) => M[k] || { as_written: "", note: "" };
const DOCS = [
  ["marriage-1911", "The marriage register, Wynberg, 3 September 1911",
   "Duplicate Original Register no. 7271, entry 160. Pablo Armando Lerena, 29, bachelor, horse-dealer, of Diep River; Mary Septima Taylor, 27, spinster, of Diep River. After banns, by Bertram W. Glynn, R.C. Priest. Both signatures are their own."],
  ["death-notice-1950", "The death notice, 28 March 1950",
   "Estate 1847/50. Item 3 — Names and Addresses of the Parents of the deceased: Father, not known. Mother, not known. Signed R. L. Theys, Executrix Nominate, present at death."],
  ["death-certificate-1950", "The death certificate, A 214036",
   "Registered at Wynberg, entry W612/50. Robert Paul LERINA. Birthplace: South America. Age 76. Race Horse Owner. Informant W. Fraser, qualification “causing burial”."],
  ["baptism-roque-1911", "Roque Luis Armando, baptised 12 June 1911",
   "Corpus Christi, Wynberg. Born 25 November 1905 — baptised at five and a half, three months before his parents married. Godparents José Bretón and María Luisa Bretón, both by proxy. Later annotations record both his marriages."],
  ["baptism-nuno-1924", "Nuno Fernando, baptised 16 November 1924",
   "Corpus Christi, Wynberg, no. 4540. Born 6 October 1924. Godparents Michael Joseph and Mary Anne Duddy — and against the mother, in the priest’s hand, Mater Anglicana."],
  ["baptism-llorens-1872", "The Buenos Aires candidate — not this family",
   "Libro de Bautismos de la Parroquia de San Miguel, 1872. Roberto Juan Roque LLORENS, born 16 August 1872, son of Roberto Llorens and Francisca Fernández, both of Spain. Sent unsolicited from Argentina; answered and set aside."],
];
---
<Base title="The papers in the house"
      eyebrow="Read 16 September 2026 — and none of it needed an archive"
      dek="Pablo Armando's own death notice — the document this archive has been chasing since it began. A marriage register. Two more death notices. Two baptisms. An English cousin's family history that gives him a different surname. All of it was on David's own disk, and none of it had been opened.">

<section class="blk">
  <div class="note" style="margin:1.4rem 0"><strong>Why this page exists, and it is not a happy
    reason.</strong> On the morning of 16 September 2026 this archive drew up a ranked list of files
    for a researcher going to Pretoria, and put the death notices of <strong>Rogue Luis Armando
    Lerena</strong> and <strong>Nuno Fernando Lerena</strong> first and second. Both had been
    photographed and saved in <em>Genealogy &amp; Family History/Lerena</em> since
    <strong>November 2021</strong>. Nobody had opened the folder. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The
    corrections &rarr;</a></div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The one that answers, and the answer is a null</h2>
  <p class="dek">This morning this archive called <strong>KAB MOOC 6/9/17017</strong> &mdash; Pablo
    Armando&rsquo;s own death notice &mdash; <em>&ldquo;the single most valuable file in this entire
    archive, because it asks for HIS parents &mdash; the one document that could name Juan Carlos
    Lerena.&rdquo;</em> There has been an open request for it at the Western Cape Archives for over a
    week. <strong>It was on the disk.</strong></p>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:22px 0">
    <div class="lab">Item 3 &mdash; Names and Addresses of the Parents of the deceased</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:12px 0 0">Father &mdash; <strong>not known</strong></p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:6px 0 0">Mother &mdash; <strong>not known</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>The informant was not family.</strong> Rose Lilian Theys signed it on 28 March 1950 as
      <em>&ldquo;Present at death&rdquo;</em> and <em>&ldquo;Executrix Nominate&rdquo;</em> &mdash; the
      woman he lived with at No. 7 Forth Road, described in the liquidation account as a spinster and
      <em>no relation</em>. She could not name his parents. <strong>Nor did any of his three living
      adult sons supply them.</strong></p>
    <p>Cheryl Defranceski wrote to her English cousin in 2020: <em>&ldquo;My Father never discussed his
      parents with me or my siblings so we never knew anything about either of them.&rdquo;</em>
      <strong>That silence is now dated. It had already closed by March 1950.</strong></p>
    <div class="note" style="margin:20px 0"><strong>So the 1908 Argentine enrolment card remains the only
      document on earth that names Juan Carlos Lerena and María Lerena.</strong> Everything this archive
      does from here rests on one card filed in a drawer in Buenos Aires, and on nothing else.
      <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>The card &rarr;</a></div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">The rest of it</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">On the form</th><th>As written</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {["2_birthplace_nationality", "4_age", "5_occupation", "6_residence", "7_status",
          "7a_surviving_spouse", "7b_predeceased_spouse", "7c_place_of_last_marriage",
          "8_date_of_death", "9_where_died", "10_children", "10_other_marriages",
          "signature", "capacity"].map((k) => (
          <tr><td class="cap">{(D[k] ? k : k).replace(/^\d+[a-z]?_/, "").replace(/_/g, " ")}</td>
            <td><span set:html={em(D[k] ? D[k].as_written : "")} />
              {D[k] && D[k].note && <><br /><span class="cap" set:html={em(D[k].note)} /></>}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>And an errand closes.</strong> The request to the
    Western Cape Archives is answered. What is still worth having out of that estate file is
    <strong>the will of 6 February 1942</strong> and the inventory &mdash; not the death notice.</div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">And a second 1950 document, which is not the same thing</h3>
  <p class="dek">A death <em>notice</em> is filed for the Master. A death <em>certificate</em> is the
    civil registration. The same folder holds <strong>Union of South Africa death certificate
    A&nbsp;214036</strong>, district of Wynberg, entry W612/50 &mdash; and it disagrees with the notice,
    which disagrees with the passport.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">On the certificate</th><th>As written</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {["cert_name", "cert_age", "cert_birthplace", "cert_occupation", "cert_cause", "cert_doctor",
          "cert_burial", "cert_informant"].map((k) => (
          <tr><td class="cap">{k.replace("cert_", "").replace(/_/g, " ")}</td>
            <td><span set:html={em(D[k] ? D[k].as_written : "")} />
              {D[k] && D[k].note && <><br /><span class="cap" set:html={em(D[k].note)} /></>}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:22px 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Not one document made at his death was filled in by anybody who knew
      where he came from.</strong> The notice was completed by a woman of no relation who was present
      when he died. The certificate was completed by <strong>W. Fraser, of Main Road, Observatory,
      whose stated qualification is &ldquo;causing burial&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; the undertaker. His
      three adult sons appear on the notice as heirs and on neither as informants.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1. The marriage, Wynberg, 3 September 1911</h2>
  <p class="dek">Duplicate Original Register no. 7271, entry 160. This archive did not have it. It sat
    on the work list as <em>&ldquo;the 1911 Taylor marriage record&rdquo;</em>.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Field</th><th>As written</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {["date", "solemnized_at", "parish", "groom", "groom_age", "groom_condition",
          "groom_profession", "groom_residence", "bride", "bride_age", "bride_condition",
          "bride_residence", "after_banns_or_licence", "officiant", "witnesses"].map((k) => (
          <tr><td class="cap">{k.replace(/_/g, " ")}</td>
            <td><span set:html={em(row(k).as_written)} />
              {row(k).note && <><br /><span class="cap" set:html={em(row(k).note)} /></>}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:24px 0">
    <div class="lab">What it cannot do, and this matters most</div>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><strong>The Cape marriage register of 1911 has no column for the fathers
      of the parties.</strong> Name, age, race, condition, profession, residence, banns, consent &mdash;
      and nothing else. So this document <em>cannot</em> name Juan Carlos Lerena, and his absence from it
      is a fact about the printed form rather than about the family.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>What it does add.</strong> Three things this archive did not have.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>He was a Catholic.</strong> Married at <strong>St Dominic&rsquo;s, Wynberg</strong>, by
        <strong>Bertram W. Glynn, R.C. Priest</strong>, after banns. It is the first religious
        affiliation this archive has for him, and it is consistent with an Argentine, Italian or
        Spanish origin.</li>
      <li><strong>&ldquo;Horse-dealer&rdquo;, in 1911.</strong> Twenty years before the
        <a href={u("/naairs/")}>1931 stables plans</a> and twenty-nine before the passport says
        <em>Trainer</em>. He began as a <em>dealer</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Both parties of Diep River.</strong> They were neighbours before they were married,
        and banns require settled local residence &mdash; so he was established at the Cape well before
        September 1911.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>And a discrepancy, recorded rather than smoothed.</strong> The register makes the bride
      <strong>27</strong> last birthday. This archive carries Mary Septima Taylor born
      <strong>17 September 1884</strong>, which would make her 26 that day &mdash; she turned 27 a
      fortnight after the wedding. Her Northam baptism of 30 October 1884 supports 1884, so the likely
      answer is that somebody rounded up to a birthday two weeks away.
      <a href={u("/taylor/")}>The Taylors &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>2. Two death notices, and what they really say</h2>
  <p class="dek">Form A, under section 7 of the Administration of Estates Act 1965, out of the Transvaal
    estate files <strong>MHG 1518/73</strong> and <strong>MHG 6746/71</strong>.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem"></th>
        <th>Rogue Luis Armando<br /><span class="cap">MHG 1518/73</span></th>
        <th>Nuno Fernando<br /><span class="cap">MHG 6746/71</span></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {["date_of_birth", "place_of_birth", "date_of_death", "place_of_death", "occupation",
          "residence", "surviving_spouse", "predeceased_spouse", "children", "father", "mother",
          "informant"].map((k) => (
          <tr><td class="cap">{k.replace(/_/g, " ")}</td>
            <td set:html={em(N[k] ? N[k].rogue_luis_armando_1518_73 : "")} />
            <td set:html={em(N[k] ? N[k].nuno_fernando_6746_71 : "")} /></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
      <div class="lab">The one that was worth R2,850</div>
      <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0">Place of birth: <strong>Cape Town</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">Rogue was born <strong>6 December 1905</strong>, and <em>not</em> in
        Argentina. The family was at the Cape by the end of 1905 &mdash; three years before the
        <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>1908 enrolment card</a> was filed at the Cape Town office, and
        twenty-three years after Pablo Armando&rsquo;s own birth at Rosario. That was exactly the
        question this archive proposed to send somebody to Pretoria to answer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c)">
      <div class="lab">And the one that was not</div>
      <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0">Parents: <strong>&ldquo;Deceased&rdquo;</strong></p>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">Nuno&rsquo;s death notice was ranked second on the promise that it would
        <em>name his parents</em>. <strong>Item 19 carries the single word &ldquo;Deceased&rdquo; and a
        ditto mark.</strong> No names at all. A death notice is only ever as good as the informant, and
        his widow either did not know or did not trouble.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><strong>What they do give.</strong> Rogue&rsquo;s notice names his father
      <strong>ROBERTO LERENA</strong> &mdash; the anglicised <em>Robert Paul</em> form, written by his own
      widow in 1973. It ties Pablo Armando and Robert Paul together in a family document rather than in a
      clerk&rsquo;s index. His mother is <strong>Mary Septima Lerena</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Neither son followed his father onto the turf.</strong> A <em>retired railwayman</em> and a
      <em>salesman</em>. The horses stopped with Pablo Armando.</p>
    <p>And Nuno&rsquo;s five children are listed, among them <strong>Cheryl Ann Lerena, minor</strong>
      &mdash; whose DNA is read on <a href={u("/dna/")}>this archive&rsquo;s DNA page</a> &mdash; and
      <strong>Catherine Tertia</strong>, <em>the third</em>, standing beside her grandmother
      <strong>Mary Septima</strong>, <em>the seventh</em>. The naming habit runs the whole length of the
      family.</p>
    <p class="cap"><strong>A discrepancy left standing.</strong> {N.file && ""}This archive&rsquo;s
      register gives Rogue&rsquo;s birth as <strong>25 November 1905</strong> from a Corpus Christi
      Wynberg baptism. The death notice and his identity number both say <strong>6 December 1905</strong>.
      Eleven days. Both are recorded; neither is quietly preferred.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>3. The page of the passport nobody turned over</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive transcribed the Argentine passport of December 1940 a week ago. It had not
    read page 6 &mdash; <em>Justificativos de Nacionalidad</em>, the proofs of nationality he actually
    laid on the consul&rsquo;s desk.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">On the booklet</th><th>What it says, and what it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {just.map((r) => (
          <tr><td class="cap">{r.field.replace("justificativos_", "").replace(/_/g, " ")}</td>
            <td><span set:html={em(r.value_as_written)} />
              <br /><span class="cap" set:html={em(r.note)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:24px 0">
    <div class="lab">The finding on that page</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0">&ldquo;Acta del Registro Civil (ó fe de bautismo) extendida
      por &#95;&#95;&#95;&#95;&#95;&#95;&rdquo; &mdash; <strong>the line is blank</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">In December 1940, applying to <em>his own consulate</em>, Pablo Armando
      produced <strong>no birth certificate and no baptismal certificate</strong>. The consul took his
      nationality on the enrolment book alone.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">This archive has read the Rosario registers <a href={u("/searched/")}>act
      by act</a> looking for his baptism and found nothing. <strong>Here is contemporary evidence that he
      did not have one to hand either.</strong> It does not prove no act exists &mdash; but the man
      himself, at fifty-eight, could not or did not produce one.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note"><strong>And a document this archive did not know existed.</strong> The proof he
    <em>did</em> produce was a <strong>libreta de enrolamiento issued by the Consulado General Argentino
    at Cape Town, dated 1915</strong>. The enrolment record this archive holds is the
    <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>ficha of 1908</a>. <strong>So there are two Argentine enrolment
    events, seven years apart, both filed at the Cape.</strong> The 1915 libreta has never been looked
    for. It is on <a href={u("/worklist/")}>the work list</a>.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>4. Two baptisms, and a family regularised</h2>
  <p class="dek">Both from <strong>Corpus Christi Catholic Church, 2 Clare Road, Wynberg</strong>, sourced
    by Cheryl Defranceski directly from the parish archives. This archive had neither.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Roque Luis Armando.</strong> <em>Anno 1905 die 25 mensis Nov. natus, et anno 1911 die 12
      mensis Junii baptizatus est &mdash; Roque Lois Armando, filius Pablo Armando Lerena et Mary Septima
      (olim Taylor) conjugum. A me Bertram W. Glynn.</em></p>
    <p><strong>Read the dates.</strong> He was born <strong>25 November 1905</strong> and baptised
      <strong>12 June 1911</strong> &mdash; at five and a half years old, and
      <strong>three months before his parents married</strong>. The priest who baptised him,
      <em>Bertram W. Glynn</em>, is the same priest who married them at St Dominic&rsquo;s that
      September, and he wrote <em>conjugum</em> &mdash; &ldquo;of the spouses&rdquo; &mdash; three months
      early.</p>
    <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:20px 0">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>That is a legitimation, and it explains the marriage register.</strong>
        The 1911 entry calls him a <em>bachelor</em> and her a <em>spinster</em> because it was their
        first marriage &mdash; with a five-year-old son already in the house. The child was baptised
        first, the parents married second, and one priest did both inside a hundred days.</p>
    </div>
    <p><strong>And the godparents are the first Hispanic names ever found around him in the Cape:</strong>
      <em>Patrinus fuit <strong>José Bretón</strong> (proxy); Matrina fuit <strong>María Luisa
      Bretón</strong> (proxy).</em> <strong>Both by proxy</strong> &mdash; which is what a parish does
      when the real godparents are overseas. Two Spanish-named sponsors, standing in absentia, at the
      baptism of an Argentine&rsquo;s first son. Nobody has ever looked for them.</p>
    <p class="cap">Later annotations on the same entry: he married <strong>Rosalina Wilhelmina
      Chappell</strong> at St Joseph&rsquo;s, Johannesburg, on 21 September 1936; and, as a widower,
      again at St Charles, Victory Park, Johannesburg, on 12 March 1966. <strong>The register confirms
      she was already a Chappell when he married her</strong> &mdash; which is the relationship the DNA
      clusters produced independently the same day.</p>
    <p><strong>Nuno Fernando</strong>, no. 4540, born <strong>6 October 1924</strong>, baptised
      <strong>16 November 1924</strong> &mdash; six weeks, not five years. Godparents
      <strong>Michael Joseph Duddy</strong> and <strong>Mary Anne Duddy</strong>. And against the mother,
      in the priest&rsquo;s hand: <strong><em>Mater Anglicana</em></strong>.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>&ldquo;The mother is Anglican.&rdquo;</strong> So the Catholicism was
      <em>his</em>. Mary Septima Taylor of Northam was Church of England, married in a Catholic church
      after banns, and her children were baptised Catholic. On the question of where Pablo Armando came
      from, that is not decoration: <strong>he was the Catholic party in a mixed marriage, and he held
      to it.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>5. What the Devon family remembered &mdash; and they called him something else</h2>
  <p class="dek">Sue Rowbotham, a second cousin in Cheltenham, wrote a Taylor family history called
    <em>Three Corners of the Land</em>. She and David found each other in April 2020. This archive has
    never read it, nor the email thread that followed between Sue and Cheryl.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <div class="lab">From the section on Mary Septima Taylor</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:12px 0 0">&ldquo;Mary Septima Taylor is said to have gone out to South
      Africa to work for a widower by the name of <strong>Robert (or Roberto) MARINO</strong> who lived
      in the Cape Town area. Roberto had three sons; Louis, Cardo and Nuno.&rdquo;</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Marino.</strong> Not Lerena, and nothing like it phonetically. The three sons are exactly
      right &mdash; Louis is Roque Luis Armando, Cardo is Ricardo Juan Carlos, Nuno is Nuno Fernando
      &mdash; so the memory is anchored in real people. It is the <em>surname</em> that has drifted, or
      that was never Lerena in the first place.</p>
    <p><strong>Marino is one of the commonest surnames in Italy.</strong> This archive has argued from
      two companies&rsquo; independent readings of one granddaughter&rsquo;s DNA that Pablo Armando
      carried Italian ancestry. Here, from an entirely separate direction, written down by an English
      historian before any DNA was tested, is a family memory that he was called
      <strong>Marino</strong>. <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></p>
    <div class="note" style="margin:18px 0"><strong>It proves nothing, and it is the best corroboration
      the Italian reading has.</strong> It could as easily be his mother&rsquo;s surname, a name he used
      in the Cape, or a Devon mishearing carried a century. All three are worth a search that has never
      been run. <strong>None of them is nothing.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The rest of the memory is wrong in an instructive way.</strong> He was no widower with
      three sons: the baptism names Mary Septima as Roque&rsquo;s mother and the register calls them
      bachelor and spinster. What the Devon family were told was the respectable version of a woman who
      went out at nineteen and had a child six years before she married.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And what it gives that no document did</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22rem">Remembered</th><th>What this archive can say</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {memory.slice(3).map((r) => (
          <tr><td><span set:html={em(r.what_was_remembered)} />
            <br /><span class="cap" set:html={em(r.source) + " &middot; " + em(r.grade)} /></td>
            <td set:html={em(r.what_this_archive_can_say)} /></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>6. A candidate sent from Argentina, answered and set aside</h2>
  <p class="dek">Filed under <em>Roberto Paul Lerena Documents</em>, between the estate papers, are two
    photographs of a <strong>Buenos Aires baptism register of 1872</strong>. This archive first wrote
    that somebody had pulled them deliberately &mdash; <strong>an intention nobody had stated.</strong>
    David says they arrived <strong>unsolicited, in the mail, from a researcher in Argentina</strong>,
    and that he regards the man as <strong>likely no relation</strong>.
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>
  <div class="note" style="margin:18px 0"><strong>So why keep it on the page at all?</strong> Three
    reasons. A candidate that arrives from a stranger still has to be <em>answered</em> rather than
    dropped. The age evidence it rests on is real and belongs on the record whatever the verdict. And
    <strong>the name has already leaked into the family tree</strong>, where he is entered as
    <em>&ldquo;Roberto Paul (Llorens) Lerena&rdquo;</em> &mdash; so the reading is in circulation, and
    is better refuted in public than left to drift.</div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <div class="lab">Libro de Bautismos de la Parroquia de San Miguel, Año de 1872</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0">Roberto Juan Roque <strong>LLORENS</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">Born <strong>16 August 1872</strong>, baptised 4 September 1872 &mdash;
      <em>hijo legítimo</em> of <strong>Don Roberto Llorens</strong>, of Spain, 25, and
      <strong>Doña Francisca Fernández</strong>, of Spain, 23, of <em>Calle de Chacabuco 156</em>.
      Godparents Juan Berenguer and Rita Carbajo, both Spanish, both of Calle de la Victoria 478.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">For</div>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0;padding-left:18px">
        {forRows.map((r) => (<li style="margin-bottom:10px"><span set:html={em(r.value)} /></li>))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">Against</div>
