        {againstRows.map((r) => (<li style="margin-bottom:10px"><span set:html={em(r.value)} /></li>))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:24px 0">
    <div class="lab">Where this archive lands</div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin:10px 0 0" set:html={em(L.verdict ? L.verdict.value : "")} />
    <p style="margin-top:12px" set:html={em(L.verdict ? L.verdict.note : "")} />
  </div>

  <div class="note"><strong>And it is cheap to settle.</strong>
    <span set:html={em(L.what_would_settle_it ? L.what_would_settle_it.value : "")} /></div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><strong>One more thing came out of that folder, and it is not this family&rsquo;s.</strong> A
      1991 burial index page for the FA&mdash; surnames, carrying <strong>Falco Carmine Antonio,
      Caterina, Filomena and Raffaele</strong>. It is misfiled here. It belongs to the Falco archive and
      it is left where it is, noted and untouched, because this estate does not mix its families.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>7. The family tree &mdash; and a source this archive should never have used</h2>
  <div class="note" style="margin:18px 0"><strong>This section replaces a retracted one.</strong>
    Earlier today this archive found a GEDCOM on the disk, mined it, and published from it. David:
    <em>&ldquo;Do not use the 2018 gedcom. That is an old backup only. And is incredibly wrong and out
    of date.&rdquo;</em> He was right. The current export &mdash; <strong>15 September 2026, 15,643
    individuals against the backup&rsquo;s 8,286</strong> &mdash; contradicts it point by point, and
    everything taken from the backup is withdrawn.
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">The 2018 backup said</th>
        <th style="width:17rem">The current tree says</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ged.map((r) => (
          <tr><td set:html={em(r.what_the_2018_backup_said)} />
            <td set:html={em(r.what_the_current_tree_says)} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={em(r.why_it_matters)} /></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:22px 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>The lesson, which this archive should not have needed.</strong>
      A file&rsquo;s <strong>date is part of its provenance</strong>, and &ldquo;found on the disk&rdquo;
      is not a source. It was treated as one because it was large and internally consistent, and it was
      neither current nor right. Worse: <strong>two errors were caught by internal arithmetic</strong>
      &mdash; an impossible birth date and a man dead at 117 &mdash; and both were written up as
      isolated slips when they were <em>symptoms</em>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>7b. The Brayley ascent, re-derived &mdash; eleven generations to 1643</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive&rsquo;s own <code>rootschat-assessment.tsv</code> says Brayley
    <em>&ldquo;is the one this archive could not push past.&rdquo;</em> The current tree pushes past it
    to <strong>Matthew Reed, born 1643 at Braunton</strong>.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Gen</th><th style="width:13rem">Person</th><th>Born · died · married</th>
        <th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ascent.map((r) => (
          <tr><td class="m">{r.gen}</td><td set:html={em(r.person)} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={em([r.born, r.died, r.married].filter(Boolean).join(" · "))} />
            <td class="cap" set:html={em(r.note)} /></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:24px 0">
    <div class="lab">And there is the Walker</div>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">This archive noticed that <strong>Walker</strong> runs through the Taylor
      line as a given name &mdash; Margaret Hellen <em>Walker</em> Taylor, Agnes <em>Walker</em> Taylor
      &mdash; and guessed a Walker grandmother. <strong>She is five generations up: HELEN WALKER, who
      married George Temple at Haddington, East Lothian, on 6 January 1813.</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">And so does the other name: <strong>Helen Walker → Mary Ann
      <em>Hellen</em> Brayley → Margaret <em>Hellen</em> Walker Taylor.</strong> Four generations of a
      Scotswoman&rsquo;s name carried into a Devon family and out to the Cape.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Which is why a South African DNA match shares
      <strong>Temple <em>and</em> Walker</strong> with Cheryl &mdash; see below. The tree and the
      chromosome arrive at the same woman from opposite ends.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note"><strong>What makes this testable rather than decorative:</strong> almost every line
    names <strong>a parish and a date</strong> &mdash; Westleigh, Coldridge, Braunton, Shirwell,
    Holsworthy, Bideford, Northam; Haddington; St Martin-in-the-Fields and St Bartholomew&rsquo;s. North
    Devon parish registers are filmed, and this archive knows how to read a filmed register by number.
    <strong>The three to test first</strong> are the Bideford marriage of 26 January 1870, the
    Westminster marriage of 9 July 1839, and the death of John Brailey at the
    <strong>Devon County Asylum</strong> on 4 May 1851 &mdash; which is very probably the
    <em>&ldquo;missing husband&rdquo;</em> of Sue Rowbotham&rsquo;s chapter title.</div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>8. The Brayley convergence, tested &mdash; and it does not go where it looked</h2>
  <p class="dek">Three roads appeared to point at Mary Septima&rsquo;s mother&rsquo;s line on the same
    day: a DNA cluster of <strong>Bailey</strong> trees; this archive&rsquo;s own note that
    <strong>Brayley</strong> was the line it &ldquo;could not push past&rdquo;; and a GEDCOM that turned
    out to carry Brayley back to <strong>1747</strong>. Here is what happened when they were put against
    each other.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:7rem">Probe</th><th class="m">Hits</th><th>What came back, and what it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {probes.map((r) => (
          <tr><td><strong set:html={em(r.probe)} /></td><td class="m">{r.hits}</td>
            <td><span set:html={em(r.what_came_back)} />
              <br /><span class="cap" set:html={em(r.reading)} /></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre,#8a7f5c);margin:24px 0">
    <div class="lab">And the part that does not work &mdash; which is the point of running it</div>
    {convFind.map((r) => (
      <p style="margin:12px 0 0"><span set:html={em(r.probe)} /> <span set:html={em(r.hits)} /></p>
    ))}
    {convJoin && <p style="margin:14px 0 0"><strong>What would join them.</strong>
      <span set:html={em(convJoin.hits)} /></p>}
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>What the convergence actually delivered.</strong> Not the Bailey cluster. Something duller
      and better: <strong>the Taylor maternal line is now corroborated in three independent
      places</strong> &mdash; a family tree that carries it back to John Brayley of 1747, an English
      cousin&rsquo;s written history that carries the same people, and a match list in which
      <strong>Brayley, Temple and Walker</strong> all turn up as shared ancestral surnames at
      fourth-to-fifth-cousin distances.</p>
    <p><strong>For Mary Septima Taylor&rsquo;s mother&rsquo;s family, this archive moved from a wall to a
      documented ascent of six generations in an afternoon. For Pablo Armando it moved nothing at
      all.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The documents themselves</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has been text since it began. These are the six it can publish: every
    person named on them is dead. <strong>The 1971 and 1973 death notices are deliberately not here</strong>
    &mdash; they list living children with their birth dates, and this estate names the living and
    nothing more.</p>
